**Chapter 10 Section 14: "Usurper"**

——The *Dragon's Eye*.

It was a phrase that intuitively flashed through Subaru's mind.

Opportunities to lay eyes upon an existence referred to as a "Dragon"—not an Earth Dragon, Water Dragon, or Flying Dragon—are few and far between. Yet, having witnessed the "Divine Dragon" Volcanica atop the Pleiades Watchtower and the "Cloud Dragon" Mezoreia in the Vollachian Empire, Subaru could be considered relatively experienced when it came to "Dragons."

However, it was not that wealth of sightings that drove Subaru's intuition. This was something rooted far more deeply in the instincts of a living being.

When you face a *Dragon*, you feel it. —A difference in the stage of life itself.

A reverence for an existence belonging to a fundamentally different hierarchy as a living creature; that is a feeling one has no choice but to experience in the presence of a "Dragon." That very sensation was the confirmation.

The sensation Subaru felt from Crusch's left eye, once her eyepatch was removed, was precisely that.

"—"

Crusch's left eye, shining gold and bearing an unearthly light—the eye of something inhuman. Faced with what could only be called a *Dragon Eye* resting in her socket, Subaru felt his heart tremble with dread.

What had happened to her? What did that left eye signify? Why wouldn't she cast aside her sword even when her wind was sealed? Why had things come to this?

The whirl of unresolvable questions churning in his mind was perhaps an instinctive defensive reflex, a fear of confronting the reality before his eyes. Swallowing such formalized reasoning, Subaru somehow managed to open his mouth—

"—Ah."

—He felt as though a gaze tinged with golden light had pierced his heart.

"—Shamak!"

In that instant, in place of the frozen Subaru, Beatrice raised her hand and incanted. Respecting Subaru's reluctance to fight Crusch, she chose Shamak, the Yin Magic that tears the target's consciousness away from reality. A spell excellent beyond compare for the purpose of neutralizing a target, she deployed it with a level of mastery incomparable to Subaru's.

It should have enveloped Crusch's head and severed her will to fight from this place. And yet—

"—I can see it."

With a tilt of her head, Crusch leisurely dodged the swirling black haze that had suddenly manifested in empty space.

"—Tch, Shamak! Shamak, Shamak, Shamak!"

Swallowing her shock at the first strike being evaded, Beatrice conjured black haze after black haze in midair in rapid succession. Yet Crusch, with agile movements, evaded every single one of the ceaselessly chaining attempts to engulf her. Finally, she matched the very emergence of the magic with her blade and destroyed the formula itself.

Crushing magic before its activation. The logic was understandable—all magic only takes effect when directionality is given to gathered mana, so disrupting its composition prevents its formation.

"But pulling that off is a whole different matter, isn't it!?" Tiga exclaimed, a mix of shock and wonder in his voice, having witnessed the divine feat of cutting down magic before it even became magic. He leaped at Crusch from the side.

Sword and kick—displaying the dexterity of attacking both simultaneously by himself—Tiga resumed his battle with Crusch, who met him with a dancing blade. However...

"I told you. I can see it," Crusch stated quietly, her movements now visibly and drastically changed, even to an ordinary observer.

The change was not only visible but clearly audible. For, unlike the battle moments before, there was something missing from their fight. Sound.

The clashing of steel on steel, the sparks-scattering ring of blades—that exchange had ceased. Crusch dodged Tiga's sword strikes, his kicks, not with her sword, but solely with her body.

Even a novice could tell which was the more poised tactic: blocking or evading. And what made such a feat possible—

"—The Dragon's Eye."

Subaru's throat gulped down a sense of wonder he couldn't suppress.

Likely, Crusch's sheer speed hadn't changed much. What had changed drastically wasn't the speed of her movement, but the swiftness of its initiation.

In a battle where lives are traded in split-second offense and defense, if one person could gain *something* that shaved off a tenth of a second from that chain of moments, their advantage would be immeasurable. What Crusch was doing now was erasing that very tenth of a second.

"Guh... Kuh...!"

As if to confirm Subaru's conjecture, the tide of the sword fight in the office shifted dramatically in an instant. Tiga's sword dance—unleashing a variety of techniques with his entire dynamism, a performance that had, to some extent, allowed him to clash head-on with Crusch—was interrupted, forcing him squarely onto the defensive.

His initial attacks were crushed, his combos pierced by her blade in their gaps, his freedom of movement sealed. The usually unflappable Tiga now bore a look of impatience on his profile, barely managing to resist.

But even a full focus on defense couldn't keep him unscathed. Blood oozed from his shoulder, his thigh, and finally, from a single line drawn across his cheek, blood trickled—the trail of dripping crimson telling the story of his uphill battle.

At this rate, Tiga would soon join the victims of the MacMahon manor.

That was the worst possible scenario that could not be allowed to happen, especially now that Miklotov had already suffered something irreversible.

Therefore—

"Oooooooh—!!"

As Tiga attempted suppression with his sword and Beatrice with her magic, Subaru roared. He lashed out with the whip he had drawn from his lower back, making the tip of Guiltywhip howl through the air.

Ever since deciding to use E・M・T, Subaru's objective had been to restrain Crusch. Leaving the earlier combat to Beatrice and the others was not solely because he was bewildered and dumbfounded by Crusch's transformation. It was for this single, singular ambush.

Faster than sword or magic, the lash of Guiltywhip closed in on Crusch. Whatever the mechanism of the Dragon Eye, dealing with an unfamiliar attack—

"—That, I have seen before."

"—Oh."

In an instant, struck by two shocks, a pathetic breath leaked from Subaru's lungs.

The first shock was having his carefully held ambush so easily thwarted. Before the whip hurled from Subaru's hand could fully extend, Crusch met it with her sword, slamming its tip to the floor. The strike that should have been the fastest in this place—having it so effortlessly cut down was worthy of astonishment. Yet, compared to what the second shock brought, it was trivial.

The second shock—it was witnessing the poignant color held in Crusch's right eye, not the Dragon Eye, but her natural amber eye, after she had slashed away Guiltywhip.

"—I don't get it."

Choosing his words bluntly, her gaze, which looked like she was about to cry, brought him only bewilderment. It was a sensation as if Subaru, trying to apprehend her, was being treated as the villain, even though it was Crusch acting strangely. Even though he'd even been killed once already, this was beyond just griping about the unfairness of a beauty's tears.

Of course, one could call it a baseless accusation. In reality, Crusch simply glanced at Subaru, the whip's wielder, with an expressionless face, her lips sealed tight. —But, somehow, he thought it. He thought she seemed about to cry.

—I'll stop those tears.

"—Tch."

"Don't lose focus—!"

Careless of Subaru, who was chewing his lip at the emotion that had crossed Crusch's eyes, Tiga—albeit interpreting the situation differently—had spotted an opening to exploit. With a ferocious move, he flashed his shamshir.

The curved sword's white blade leaped, howling like a beast's fang as it closed in on Crusch's slender body—

"I said I can see it!"

In a split second, Crusch twisted her body to match the trajectory of the flashing curved blade. Evading the strike such that it merely grazed her skin, she raised her voice in anger and drove her springing knee squarely into Tiga's jaw.

A muffled "Gah!" of anguish escaped Tiga as he reeled backward. Blades of light rained down upon him mercilessly, spattering his blood across the office as he frantically protected his vital spots. His feet stumbled back involuntarily, and then a ferocious kick plowed into his torso. Blown straight back, Tiga slammed back-first into the wall with a thunderous crash and was suppressed.

However—

"I'll pick up the pieces, my friend!"

Using the scant time Tiga had bought him, Subaru closed in on Crusch. He aimed a whip strike at her sword hand as he drew near—

"I can see that too."

Uttering curtly, Crusch avoided the whip with only a flick of her wrist. In the same motion, her flipping blade turned into a thrust, aimed at the leg of the recklessly approaching Subaru. The silver flash would pierce the top of his foot, forcing Subaru to pay the price for his reckless full-body charge—

"Beatrice!"

"—Tch! You're a partner who asks for nothing but reckless things, I suppose!"

At his single shout, Beatrice snapped a retort, sensing Subaru's intent. She used her entire small body to its fullest to grant her partner's wish.

It activated in the moment the blade was to land. —The Absolute Defense Magic, E・M・M.

"—"

Subaru and Beatrice's original magic. By shifting the user's existence half a step from reality, the ultimate defense that rejects all physical and magical interference activated. That said, pulling off the reckless trick of using E・M・M inside an active E・M・T was a first—success would be a miracle...

"Shall I mandate morning, noon, and night love calls as the price!"

"I love you, Beako!"

As he paid into the adorable fee demanded for the miracle, Subaru immediately switched gears from the fact that the thrust had no effect, targeting Crusch as she readied her next move.

E・M・M, in exchange for its impregnable defense, had the flaw of rendering movement impossible. The moment he took his next step, the invincibility would end. The next blade would pierce without mercy. Therefore—

"Invisible Providence!"

Before the next strike could be unleashed, Subaru rapidly played his hand.

From Subaru's chest, where a nosebleed spurted, an invisible black arm extended. It seized the arm of Crusch holding her sword, forcibly stopping the follow-up attack she was about to unleash. The unseen arm gripped her wrist tightly, forcing the sword from her hand and stripping away her armed might.

Consecutively thwarted by hand after unknown hand of Subaru's, Crusch's remaining arm was also pounced upon as Subaru threw his whole body onto it. With the Unseen Hand and his own two arms—effectively three arms—he pinned her against the wall.

"Crusch-san! Please, listen to me!"

Gripping both her wrists, forcibly holding Crusch against the wall, Subaru appealed to her.

Even then, Crusch twisted her body, trying to drive a knee into Subaru's flank. But he pushed his body further in aggressively, pressing close enough to feel her breath and sealing her movements.

Right before his eyes, Crusch's two-colored eyes—amber and gold—stared at him. Meeting them head-on, Subaru shouted:

"This... this is messed up, no matter how you look at it! There's a misunderstanding! I won't let things proceed like this!"

"Natsuki Subaru..."

"I... I won't accept any of this! I won't accept it!!"

At Subaru's loud roar, Crusch's cheeks tensed.

In the end, the gist of what he was saying was the same as what he had cried out before *Return by Death*. Even so, he believed that standing here forcing her to think would yield a different result from just standing still and wailing like last time.

Miklotov's fate, the lives of those at the MacMahon manor—they wouldn't return. Even so, there had to be another way besides just sitting back and accepting the worst. For that, it was absolutely essential that Crusch lower her sword here.

Persuasion for that purpose—

"—Eh?"

Filled with such hope, waiting for Crusch's reaction, Subaru's throat leaked a breathy gasp. The reason was the event before his eyes—the sudden sharp glint in Crusch's mismatched eyes, and her using the recoil of bouncing her back off the wall to forcibly drag Subaru down.

Caught completely off guard by the surprise-like maneuver, Subaru was pitifully collapsed to the spot. —However, that wasn't a counterattack against Subaru. Rather, it was the opposite.

—Because in the next instant, a blaze of incredible heat swallowed Subaru's field of vision in crimson.




...




